explain.narrative:
## 1. An array of structures

If a program needs `count` records, it can allocate `count * sizeof *records` bytes and treat the result as an array of structures. Each element is a complete structure object, and the program must check the allocation and keep all indexing within `0` through `count - 1`.

## 2. Pointer members are separate resources

Consider:

```c
struct Student {
    char *name;
    int *scores;
    size_t score_count;
};
```

Allocating one `struct Student` creates storage for the structure members themselves. The `name` and `scores` members are pointer objects; they do not cause character or integer arrays to be allocated automatically. The program must allocate those separately and store their addresses in the members.

## 3. Initialize before nested allocation

Immediately after allocating the outer structure, set pointer members to `NULL` and counts to a safe value. Then allocate each nested resource and check each result before using it. This makes it possible to clean up a partially constructed structure safely if a later allocation fails.

## 4. Define destruction order

Release each nested allocation owned by the structure, then release the outer structure. A destructor-like helper can centralize this rule. Do not free a pointer member unless the structure owns the allocation it points to; a borrowed pointer must not be released by the borrower.

## 5. Partial construction is a normal failure case

If the first nested allocation succeeds but the second fails, the first still needs to be freed. Initialize every owned pointer to `NULL` and make the cleanup function tolerate `NULL`. This prevents error handling from becoming a separate source of invalid frees.

## 6. Choose the representation deliberately

A single contiguous allocation can improve locality and simplify cleanup for fixed-layout arrays. Separate nested allocations allow members to have independent sizes and lifetimes but require more careful cleanup and may involve more allocation calls. Choose based on access patterns, ownership, resizing and the complexity the program can safely maintain.

examples.code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

struct Student {
    char *name;
    int *scores;
    size_t score_count;
};

void destroy_student(struct Student *student) {
    if (student == NULL) return;
    free(student->scores);
    free(student->name);
    free(student);
}

int main(void) {
    struct Student *student = malloc(sizeof *student);
    if (student == NULL) return 1;
    student->name = NULL;
    student->scores = NULL;
    student->score_count = 0;

    student->name = malloc(sizeof "Mina");
    if (student->name == NULL) {
        destroy_student(student);
        return 1;
    }
    memcpy(student->name, "Mina", sizeof "Mina");

    student->score_count = 3;
    student->scores = malloc(student->score_count * sizeof *student->scores);
    if (student->scores == NULL) {
        destroy_student(student);
        return 1;
    }
    student->scores[0] = 78;
    student->scores[1] = 84;
    student->scores[2] = 91;

    printf("%s: %d %d %d\n", student->name,
           student->scores[0], student->scores[1], student->scores[2]);
    destroy_student(student);
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Product {
    int code;
    double price;
};

int main(void) {
    size_t count = 3;
    struct Product *products = malloc(count * sizeof *products);
    if (products == NULL) {
        fputs("allocation failed\n", stderr);
        return 1;
    }

    products[0] = (struct Product){ 101, 19.50 };
    products[1] = (struct Product){ 102, 25.00 };
    products[2] = (struct Product){ 103, 12.75 };

    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        printf("%d: %.2f\n", products[i].code, products[i].price);
    }

    free(products);
    return 0;
}
